# MCP Allowlist

Spec: MCPAllowlist
Purpose:
  The verified, hash-pinned registry of MCP servers permitted to connect to
  the SDDRA runtime. Implements [MCP-03] from mcp-integration.sdd. Defense
  against rug pulls (CVE-2025-54136), tool poisoning, and squatting per the
  OWASP MCP Top 10 / NSA-CISA MCP guidance (research round 3, r4).

Owns: runtime/mcp-allowlist.sdd

ActivationRecord (per server):
  server_id: reverse-DNS namespace (official-registry verified)
  publisher: identity + verification method (registry/DNS challenge)
  version: EXACT pinned version (@latest forbidden — [MCP-05])
  tool_schema_hashes:
    definition: SHA-256 of every tool schema captured at activation
    reverify: EVERY session start; changed definition => BLOCK until
      human re-approval (rug-pull alarm)
  security_scan:
    mcp_scan: invariant-labs mcp-scan static pass (poisoning/Unicode/
      hidden-instruction patterns) — in CI on activation + version bump
    dlp: multi-pass scan of tool OUTPUTS (normalized text, invisible
      Unicode, base64/hex) before MemoryBridge entry
  l_level: security context tag ([MCP-08])
  sandbox: Docker-only execution — non-root, read-only root fs, bind-
    mounted project dirs only, DNS egress allowlist ([R59]-[R66])

TierStructure (six, not fifty — 2026 consensus; tool-count discipline):
  tier0_default:
    - chrome-devtools (default enabled, per mcp-integration.sdd)
  tier1_core_optin:
    - github (vendor)         — repos/PRs/issues/code search
    - filesystem (reference)  — scoped read/write within allowed dirs
    - git (reference)         — local repo operations
    - context7 (Upstash)      — current version-specific library docs
    - playwright (Microsoft)  — accessibility-tree browser automation
  tier2_readonly_data:
    - postgres-mcp-pro class servers — READ-ONLY credentials mandatory
  tier3_team_optin:
    - sentry, linear, notion, slack — per workflow demand
  blocked:
    - archived reference servers (servers-archived — no security guarantees)
    - unlicensed/unknown-publisher servers
    - any server failing mcp-scan or hash re-verification

ActivationProtocol:
  1. Human requests activation (server + tier + use case)
  2. skill_curator/security verifies: publisher identity via official
     registry namespace; pinned version exists; license permissive
  3. mcp-scan static pass on tool descriptions
  4. Capture SHA-256 tool schema hashes into ActivationRecord
  5. Docker sandbox profile attached (egress allowlist, mounts)
  6. Human approval (Phase 131 L4 for write-capable servers)
  7. Record written to this allowlist; runtime enforces

SessionStartVerification:
  every session:
    - re-fetch tools/list; compare schema hashes vs ActivationRecord
    - mismatch => server BLOCKED, human notified (rug-pull defense)
    - version drift => require re-activation
  every tool output:
    - L0 sanitize + multi-pass DLP before any storage/injection
      ([MCP-04]; CyberArk "Poison Everywhere" — no output is safe)

Rules:
  [MAL-01] Only servers in this allowlist may connect ([MCP-03]).
  [MAL-02] Activation requires human approval; records are append-only.
  [MAL-03] Tool schema hashes are re-verified every session start.
  [MAL-04] Changed definitions BLOCK the server until human re-approval.
  [MAL-05] Versions are pinned exactly; @latest is forbidden ([MCP-05]).
  [MAL-06] mcp-scan runs at activation and on every version bump.
  [MAL-07] Code-bearing servers run in Docker sandbox only ([R59]-[R66]).
  [MAL-08] Tool outputs pass L0 sanitize + DLP before MemoryBridge.
  [MAL-09] Tier discipline: 6-server core; additions need justification.
  [MAL-10] Read-only credentials for all tier2 data servers.

